lick knight of brown coal has long been  at the center of environmental protests  called the dirtiest form of gold it has  been providing power to the german  economy and households for decades under  pressure from the green party the new  government announced it aims to close  these open pit mines by 2030 but that  was before russia invaded ukraine  it's clear that a terrible war in  ukraine means we are realizing that our  electricity supply in germany wasn't and  isn't very secure and there are several  possible risk factors that applies to  gas oil and to hard coal too and there  are of course some who are now thinking  there's a domestic energy source which  will always be available as well as wind  and sun of course and that's lignite to  make sure there's enough energy in  future winters that regional government  in brandenburg has asked permission to  keep some lignite-powered plants on  standby  to have the necessary supply for the  coming winter season  and that hopefully without any  dependence of imports from russia that  problem has still not been solved  and  in turn as as i'm coming from the field  of industrial safety i would prefer to  have a situation with belt and parachute  and so it would be worthwhile to keep  them  available  ukraine has accused germany of financing  russia's war because of its dependency  on russian gas oil and coal the eu has  decided to ban coal imports which will  cost russia around 4 billion euros a  year in comparison a ban on russian gas  and oil would cost the country 80  billion euros  this is one of the few power stations in  germany not depending on imports from  russia domestically produced lignite is  one of the most reliable sources germany  has but also one of the most polluting  it was unthinkable just a few months ago  that the new government in which greens  play an important role would even  consider using more coal but the war in  ukraine has changed everything and the  climate could be another victim  more co2 emissions instead of less an  uncomfortable reality for environmental  groups who have long campaigned for an  end to coal use  germany for a long period of time has  sponsored the russian regime the  oligarchy in russia and has put russia  in a position to finance that war  unfortunately we cannot get rid of that  dependency overnight it's obviously a  bitter pill we have old cold power  plants that have to get back on the grid  but we have to accelerate the build out  of renewables this needs some years but  the german industry now has a clear  target that its future is based on  renewable energy but building wind farms  and solar panels takes time and germany  is under pressure to act now that  pressure will only grow if terrible  images continue to come out of ukraine  and the eu decides to go further and ban  russian oil and gas step faster than al  jazeera in brandenburg
 

